course: adult-life
lesson: 16
title: New partners and step-families
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After parents split up, or after a mum or dad has died, a grown-up may meet someone new and fall in love again. That new person is called their partner.
explain: Grown-ups usually wait until the relationship is serious before the children meet the new partner, and they pick a relaxed, ordinary place for the first meeting.
explain: In Leeds, Dad takes Ellie for pizza and says, "There's someone I'd like you to meet." Sarah is waiting at the table, a bit nervous too, and she has two kids of her own who will come round at weekends.
explain: A new partner does not replace your mum or dad. If they marry or move in, they become a step-parent, their children become step-brothers and step-sisters, and a baby they have together is a half-brother or half-sister.
explain: Families come in many shapes: two mums, two dads, one parent, step-families, and all of them are real families. Mixing two families takes time, and it is normal to find it hard at first.
fact: When Dad says "There's someone I'd like you to meet", he means he has a new partner he cares about and wants you to know.
fact: A first meeting with a parent's new partner is often in a relaxed place like a pizza restaurant, so there is less pressure.
fact: When Mum says "We're just friends", she means they are friends, not boyfriend and girlfriend, and you can believe her.
fact: A parent's new partner is not a new mum or dad; if they marry or live together, they become a step-parent.
fact: Many children call a step-parent by their first name, and it is fine to ask what to call them.
fact: The children of a parent's partner can become your step-siblings: brothers or sisters through marriage, not by birth.
fact: A step-sister who snaps "You're not my real sister!" is often upset about changes in her own family and taking it out on you.
fact: Remarrying means marrying again after a divorce or after a husband or wife has died.
fact: Being asked to be part of a parent's wedding shows that you are an important part of the new family.
fact: A step-mum who says "I'm not trying to replace your mum" means she wants a different kind of relationship with you, not to take your mum's place.
fact: Some children have two mums or two dads because their parents are a same-sex couple, and that family is just as real as any other.
fact: A single parent looks after the children mostly alone, maybe after a separation, a death or by choice, and it is a complete family.
fact: A baby who shares only one parent with you is your half-brother or half-sister.
fact: It is normal to feel shy, curious, jealous or cross about a new partner, and you do not have to love them straight away.
exercise: Dad says, "There's someone I'd like you to meet." What does he most likely mean? | He has a new partner he wants you to get to know | He has found you a new teacher for maths after school | A new neighbour has moved in next door | He wants you to meet his new boss
exercise: Why do grown-ups often choose a pizza place for the first meeting with a new partner? | It is relaxed and ordinary, so there is less pressure | Restaurants are the only place it is allowed | The new partner has to pay for the meal | So nobody can talk about anything important
exercise: You ask Mum about the man from work and she says, "We're just friends." What does it mean? | They are friends, not boyfriend and girlfriend | They are secretly getting married | She does not like him very much | He is her new boss at work
exercise: You wonder, "Is he going to be my new dad?" What is true? | He can become a step-dad, but your dad stays your dad | Yes, he becomes your dad instead | No, you must never speak to him at all | Only if you call him Dad from now on
exercise: Dad's partner has two kids who stay at weekends. What could they become to you? | Step-brothers or step-sisters | Half-brothers or half-sisters | Cousins on Dad's side | Your adopted brothers and sisters
exercise: Your step-sister shouts, "You're not my real sister!" What is the most likely reason? | She is upset about changes in her own family | She has always hated you for years and years | She wants you to move out of the house | She does not know what a sister is
exercise: Your step-sister snaps at you again and again for weeks. What is a good thing to do? | Do not argue back, and tell a grown-up | Snap back louder and louder so she stops | Hide her things until she says she is sorry | Keep it a secret from everyone in the house
exercise: Mum is getting remarried. What does "remarried" mean? | Married again after a divorce or a death | Married to the same person twice, on the same day | Engaged but not married yet | Married in a church and a town hall
exercise: Mum asks you to be part of her wedding. What does that show? | You are an important part of the new family | You have to pay for part of the wedding with your savings | She has nobody else to ask | You must now call her partner Dad
exercise: Your step-mum says, "I'm not trying to replace your mum." What does she mean? | She knows you have a mum and wants a different bond | She will soon become your real mum and take her place | She does not want to see you very much | She wants you to stop seeing your mum
exercise: Your friend has two mums. Which is true? | It is a real family, just as much as any other | It is only pretend, not a real family | One of them must really be an aunt who lives with them | It means the friend has no dad at all ever
exercise: Your friend lives with just their dad. Which is true? | It is a complete family with a single parent | The family is only half a family | The dad must be looking after them for a while until Mum comes back | The friend must be very unhappy at home
exercise: Dad and his partner have a baby. What is the baby to you? | Your half-brother or half-sister | Your step-brother or step-sister | Your cousin | Not related to you at all
exercise: You feel cross the first time you meet Mum's new partner. Is that okay? | Yes, feelings like that are normal and it takes time | No, you must love him straight away from the first day | No, it means you are being very naughty | Yes, and you never have to be polite to him
exercise: What do many children call a step-parent? | Their first name, after asking what feels right | Always Mum or Dad, by law | Mister or Missus and their surname, always | Nothing at all, they never speak to them
exercise: Can a child love both a mum and a step-mum? | Yes, liking one does not betray the other | No, you can only ever love one mum at a time | Only if the mum gives written permission | Only once you are a grown-up
test: Overheard on the phone: "I think it's time the kids met Tom, it's getting serious." What is going on? | Mum's new partner is about to meet the children | Mum is hiring a babysitter called Tom | Tom is a new boy in the children's class | Mum is in trouble at work with Tom
test: Your little brother says, "Dad's girlfriend is our new mum now, so Mum's not our mum any more." Put him right. | Mum is still our mum; Dad's partner could be a step-mum | He's right, the new partner replaces Mum | He's right, but only at weekends | Mum stops being Mum only if they marry
test: Gossip at the school gate: "His mum's remarrying, poor kid's got a whole new lot of siblings." What new siblings are meant? | Step-siblings, the children of Mum's new husband | Half-siblings, born to his own mum and dad after the wedding | Cousins who live with him now | Twins who have just been born
test: Kayla in Toronto has one mum and one step-mum who married her mum. A boy says, "That's not a proper family." Who is right? | Kayla, every loving family is a real family | The boy, families need a mum and a dad to be proper | The boy, unless they live in the same house | Nobody, it depends on the country
test: Your mate goes, "ugh his kid's in MY room every weekend now, I didn't ask for this." Which is closest to how he feels? | Upset that blending families means sharing space | Happy to have a new best friend to play with every weekend | Glad to be moving to a bigger house | Bored because nothing has changed at home
test: Dad says, "Me and Jo are having a baby." What will the baby be to you? | A half-sibling, because you share Dad | A step-sibling, because Jo is not your mum and never will be | A cousin, because it is Jo's baby | Nothing, because you live with Mum
test: Auntie to Mum in the kitchen, a bit sharp: "So is it serious with Mark or just a fling?" What is she asking? | Whether Mum's relationship with Mark will last | Whether Mark is good at sports | Whether Mark is Mum's new boss at the office in town | Whether Mum and Mark are cousins
test: Your step-brother mutters, "shut up, you're not even my real brother." What is most likely behind it? | He is unsettled by the changes in his family | He has found out you are adopted | He has been told to say it by a parent | He wants to be your best friend
test: Lucas in Auckland lives with just his mum since his dad died. Which is true? | His is a complete one-parent family | He must be adopted by a new dad soon | It is only a family once Mum remarries | He has no real family at all any more
test: Your sister insists, "If Mum marries Dave we'll have to call him Dad, that's the rule." Put her right. | There is no such rule; many children use the first name | Yes, it is the law once they marry, and you must say it every day | Yes, but only in front of visitors | No, you are not allowed to talk to him at all
test: Mum gives you a choice: "Would you like to walk with me at the wedding, or just sit with Gran?" What does that show? | She wants you included and lets you choose | She does not really want you there at the wedding at all | You have to do whatever the partner says | You are not invited to the party after
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship of a parent's new spouse to that parent's existing child? | The spouse becomes a step-parent, not supplanting the child's other parent | The spouse becomes the child's legal and biological parent in every respect | The spouse has no relationship whatsoever to the child | The spouse replaces the other parent in every respect
exam: A child born to the child's father and the father's new partner shall hereinafter be designated as which of the following? | A half-sibling of the child | A step-sibling of the child | A cousin of the child | A foster sibling of the child
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the term "remarriage" as applied to a parent. | A further marriage following divorce or bereavement | The repetition of vows with an existing spouse upon an anniversary | A formal engagement preceding marriage | The dissolution of an existing marriage
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning families headed by two mothers. | Such a family is as genuine and complete as any other | Such a family is not recognised as a family | Such a family is temporary by its nature | Such a family requires a father to be complete
